msm: keep DSI commands and command mode frames apart.

Dragging the brightness slider flickers the panel (docs/controls-20260923.md):
each backlight DCS command collides with a frame transfer, with DSI FIFO and
timeout errors. #192's wait for DSI_STATUS0.CMD_MODE_MDP_BUSY was not enough:
a kicked-off command mode frame waits for the panel's tear signal before it
transfers, so the controller reads idle, the command starts, and the frame
starts under it. Downstream sends the backlight inside the display commit,
before the kickoff, which serializes the two by design.

Here the command mode encoder publishes its pending frame count
(pending_kickoff_cnt, decremented and woken in the pingpong-done interrupt)
in msm_drm_private while enabled. A DSI command takes cmd_xfer_lock, waits
up to 50 ms for pending frames to drain, and is sent; a kickoff takes the
same lock, so no frame starts while a command is on the link. The kickoff
runs under the commit's CRTC mutexes in process context, and the DSI path
never takes those, so the order cannot invert.

--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/msm_drv.h
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/msm_drv.h
@@ -77,6 +77,19 @@
 	struct msm_kms *kms;
 	int (*kms_init)(struct drm_device *dev);
 
+	/*
+	 * A command mode panel takes frames and DSI commands over one link. A
+	 * kicked-off frame waits for the panel's tear signal before it
+	 * transfers, so the controller's busy flag cannot keep a command out
+	 * of its way, and the two collide (DSI FIFO and timeout errors, a
+	 * flickering panel). The command mode encoder publishes its pending
+	 * frame count here while enabled: a DSI command waits for it to drain
+	 * and holds new kickoffs off until it has been sent.
+	 */
+	struct mutex cmd_xfer_lock;
+	atomic_t *cmd_pending_frames;
+	wait_queue_head_t *cmd_frames_wq;
+
 	/* subordinate devices, if present: */
 	struct platform_device *gpu_pdev;
 
